A||||7||SLYGUY||Clutch went sour again...... :(||||5||
Z||000000||SLYGUY||11-05-2001||03:33 AM||||   I had a problem as you may remember reading about a month ago with the clutch not fully disengaging on my 88GT. It turned out to be a worn out master cylinder. I replaced it with a new GM unit and all appeared okay. Well, the problem is back with a vengeance. It's at the ragged edge of not disengaging again.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>    The clutch pedal does not appear to be bent and I am not loosing fluid from either side of the master cylinder. The fluid is at the proper level although it appears dark when it shouldn't because it is new. Has my slave cylinder gone south for the winter?<P>As always your help is greatly appreciated!!<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||MrPBody||11-05-2001||07:09 AM||||Based on recent experience, I suggest checking to see if your clutch pedal returns fully to its stop.  If not, you may need to lengthen your master cylinder pushrod.  This may sound screwy, but it worked for me.<P>If the problem occurred suddenly, you might also check for a cracked slave cylinder bracket.  I haven't seen one of these, but I hear it happens.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><I>(Edit)</I> Slave cylinder failure is also possible, although -- if it wants to stay in the country -- it can't go much farther south than San Berdoo.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by MrPBody (edited 11-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Voytek||11-05-2001||11:07 AM||||Has this happened fairly suddenly?<P>Ok.  This is the problem I've had once:<BR>The spring inside the master cylinder broke.  You could NOT tell from the outside that anything was wrong.  There were no leaks and the pedal returned to its original position (or very, very close).<P>Does your pedal feel a bit soft or spongy?  This could be your problem.  The return movement in the piston inside the master (and slave) cylinder is aided by a spring.  If the spring broke, you'll only get a partial return of the spring and visually, the whole system will appear OK.<P>What I did?  I took out the master cylinder and checked for pressure just by pushing the rod by hand.  There should be pressure right from the start.  The same can be done with the slave cylinder.  It turned out that the spring inside the master was broken into 3 pieces.  Why?  I don't know.  I ended up going to a spring shop (they make ALL KINDS of springs - mattress, industrial, etc.)  I told them about the application and gave them the old spring (pieces) for proper length.  They made me 4 springs for $30 CDN (a new master cyl is about $130 CDN).<P>I hope this helps - other than a faulty clutch, this is all I can think of if you don't have any leaks.<P>Oh yeah, one more thing.  To get the piston out of the cylinder, I took the assembly to a gas station and blew it out with compressed air (tire pump).<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||SLYGUY||11-05-2001||11:50 AM||||Thanks so much for your help. <BR>No, the problem didn't happen suddenly. The point of disengagement just got closer and closer to the floor. I think the master cylinder is okay so I'm gonna:<P>1) Make sure mounting bolts on both master and slave cylinders are tight. and<BR>2) Bleed the system again. <P>Number 2 is the scary part because the last time I did that I lost the clutch completely but with a master cylinder that is only a month old that eases my mind a little.<P>I'll keep you posted<BR>Thanks again.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000004||SLYGUY||11-05-2001||11:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MrPBody:<BR><B>If not, you may need to lengthen your master cylinder pushrod.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is another option that I'm gonna pursue. I think a hacksaw and my local hardware for some pieces that I would need will help this!!<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||11-05-2001||10:03 PM||||Sorry, I did not thoroughly RTFQ.  I had a problem with a first-design master cylinder on my '85.  Your '88 has a second-design cylinder, so my solution may be irrelevant to your problem.  It's worth checking, but slave cylinder may be more likely.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||DR650SE||11-05-2001||10:23 PM||||I had a similair problem.  I figued out what it was while bleeding my system.  THe slave cylinder had a hairline crack in it.  It was one of the aftermarket ones and now I know from experiance that they are crap.  It wasn't leaking but still able to suck in air.  I only found out when I was tighting the bleeding valve and the side of it fell off.  Now I need to get a slave cylinder.  Hope you don't have the same problem.<P>------------------<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||8
Z||000007||stsmithgt||11-06-2001||09:59 AM||||I had a similar problem.  It felt like the clutch pedal was bending and then the noise came.... My clutch fork failed for some reason.||||reg||1
